      "writtenOn": "2024-10-02T15:52:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I would rework this function such that it doesn\u0027t take a callback, and I would make my own. You can probably do it without a flag too. Something like:\n```\nvoid getFolderItems(int playerId, String mediaId) {\n    // Or, just make an multi-line lambda, but I couldn\u0027t remember the syntax!\n    mMediaPlayerList.getFolderItems(\n            playerId, mediaId, (a, b) -\u003e handleFolderItemsResponse(a, b));\n}\n\nvoid handleFolderItemsResponse(String mediaId, ArrayList\u003cListItem\u003e items) {\n    // Step 1: store artwork if version 1.6 AND VFS art is enabled\n    // The cover art logic needs a flag probably\n    if (mAvrcpCoverArtService !\u003d null \u0026\u0026 mIsVfsCoverArtEnabled) {\n        for (ListItem item : items) {\n            if (item !\u003d null \u0026\u0026 item.song !\u003d null \u0026\u0026 item.song.image !\u003d null) {\n                String imageHandle \u003d \n                        mAvrcpCoverArtService.storeImage(item.song.image);\n                if (imageHandle !\u003d null) {\n                    item.song.image.setImageHandle(imageHandle);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Step 2: Send the items back down the native stack\n    mNativeInterface.getFolderItemsResponse(mediaId, items);\n}\n```\n\nThis would then require you to change AvrcpNativeInterface to no longer pass the simple callback it sends. Minus the cover art, this is a mechanical refactor in my mind. It doesn\u0027t mess with the abstraction at all, and keeps all the AVRCP logic in AVRCP. The tests need updated after though 😞",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 496,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 499,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "4d06ed95604b137fc71525f602d4664bff861a97",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
